            [
              143.0,
              1118.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "The pattern of changing $A$, opposite to that of changing $S$ (Eq. (10)), is shown in Fig. 12 in the form of a relative sea-level curve. When $y_i$ takes a low magnitude (e.g. 0.05–0.20 km), the relative sea-level change is characterized with a rapid rise in the early stage followed by a slow rise or nearly a standstill in the late stage. This trend is also seen in the case of $y_i = 0.45 \\pm 0.10$ km. When $y_i = 0.80$ km, there was no change in $A$ while the shelf edge was prograding from site $i$ to site $M$. With $y_i \\geq 0.85$ km, the relative sea-level curve is sigmoidal in shape where the highest magnitude of $A$ is realized between sites P and M.",
          "block_bbox": [
