        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "There is no overlap between $\\eta_{\\mathrm{P}}$ and $\\eta_{\\mathrm{M}}$ (i.e. $\\rho_{\\mathrm{PM}}=0$) when $y_{\\mathrm{i}}\\leq0.35$ km and $y_{\\mathrm{i}}\\geq1.05$ km. With $y_{\\mathrm{i}}=0.40-1.00$ km, on the other hand, $\\eta_{\\mathrm{P}}$ and $\\eta_{\\mathrm{M}}$ overlap each other (i.e. $0<\\rho_{\\mathrm{PM}}<1$). However, the highest value of $\\rho_{\\mathrm{PM}}$ (at $y_{\\mathrm{i}}=0.40$ km) is as low as 0.137. Excluding the cases that $y_{\\mathrm{i}}=0.40-0.45$ km, the overlap is negligible ($\\rho_{\\mathrm{PM}}\\approx0$). On the other hand, the overlap of $\\eta_{\\mathrm{M}}$ and $\\eta_{\\mathrm{D}}$ is in a significantly different trend from that between $\\eta_{\\mathrm{P}}$ and $\\eta_{\\mathrm{M}}$. There is some overlap between $\\eta_{\\mathrm{M}}$ and $\\eta_{\\mathrm{D}}$ ($0<\\rho_{\\mathrm{MD}}$) when $y_{\\mathrm{i}}=0.20-0.90$ km. At any magnitude of $y_{\\mathrm{i}}$ within this range, $\\rho_{\\mathrm{MD}}$ is much larger than that of $\\rho_{\\mathrm{PM}}$ (Fig. 14). The highest values of $\\rho_{\\mathrm{MD}}$ (0.55-0.60) are seen when $y_{\\mathrm{i}}=0.50-0.70$ km. With $y_{\\mathrm{i}}=0.45\\pm0.10$ km, $\\rho_{\\mathrm{MD}}$ ranges between 0.31 and 0.55, still much larger than the highest of $\\rho_{\\mathrm{PM}}$.",
